{
 "spec": "002 brain engine: resting-state ignition diagnostic (Phase 2, 2026-10-21)",
 "template": "female-fafb783-t2",
 "condition": "production brain at rest (no sensory input), modulation on, dt 0.5 ms; 16-24 flies (seeds 7000+, 9100+, 11000+); rates over the last 1 s",
 "findings": [
  "Below ~0.7 mV kick each fly's brain is either near-silent (~0.1 Hz central) or ignited (~20 Hz); ignition is abrupt (0.5 -> 9 Hz population rate in ~30 ms), happens at a random time and never reverts (8 s runs). The 1.5 mV production setting is the ignited state.",
  "Ignition starts in antennal-lobe local (ALLN) and projection (ALPN) neurons and spreads along the olfactory pathway; Kenyon cells carry 40% of the extra spikes but silencing them does not prevent it.",
  "Silencing all ALLN output, or the 166 excitatory-signed ALLN, removes ignition. Re-signing neurons by FlyWire known_nt / type consensus does not.",
  "The chemical edges from excitatory ALLN onto ALPN sum to ~44k weight units, as large as all ORN->ALPN input (~46k). Measured eLN->PN coupling is electrical (Yaksi & Wilson 2010, Neuron 67:1034). Setting those edges to 0 removes the two-state behaviour: at 1.0-1.5 mV every fly has a 2.3-4.1 Hz central mean and Kenyon cells 0.01-0.15 Hz (in vivo ~0.1 Hz, Turner et al. 2008). ALLN keep ~125 Hz among themselves (remaining limitation)."
 ],
 "decision": "brain.electrical.excitatory_pairs = [ALLN>ALPN] (production variant), approved by the project owner 2026-10-21; brain.noise.w to be recalibrated afterwards with the [1, 4] Hz central rule.",
